1 RUN

   Loads a VAX/VMS executable image, activates the shareable images it
   was linked against, and runs it.

   Format

     RUN filespec

2 Parameter

 filespec

   The image, on a mounted volume or on the host, by MACRO's rules for
   files (see MACRO Parameter): a VMS file specification
   (DUA1:[PROGS]HELLO.EXE), or a bare name while SET DEFAULT is on a
   volume, is a file on the volume, with the type EXE if it has none.
   Anything else, or any name followed by /HOST, is a host file; quote
   it to keep its case.  An image made on a VAX can be copied to the
   host in binary mode, or read in place from its disk.

2 Description

   The image is started at its transfer address.  A shareable image
   (one LINK/SHAREABLE made) has none, and running one is %DCL-E-NOTFR,
   "no transfer address", as on VMS.  RUN needs a VMINIT and the
   microkernel loaded and initialized, as STARTUP.COM does.

   RUN gives the image no command text: LIB$GET_FOREIGN returns an empty
   string.  To pass an image text, run it as a foreign command (see
   FOREIGN) or with MCR, or give the text after the file name on govax's
   own command line ("govax run forth.exe 2 3 + .").

2 Shareable_images

   Each shareable image the program names (in its shareable image list,
   and those images' own lists in turn) is found, checked, and placed in
   the process's P0 space before the program runs, as VMS's image
   activator does for images that aren't installed.  An image is found by
   these rules, in order:

     1. An image govax stands for (LIBRTL, LIBRTL2, MTHRTL, the system's
        run-time libraries, ...) is govax's own routines, unless a
        logical name of the image's own name is defined: DEFINE LIBRTL
        DUA1:[X]LIBRTL.EXE asks for that file instead.
     2. The image's name parsed with the default SYS$SHARE:.EXE, as VMS's
        activator does: a logical name of the image's name (DEFINE MYSHR
        DUA1:[LIB]MYSHR.EXE) leads to its file, with any fields it lacks
        taken from SYS$SHARE; otherwise SYS$SHARE does, each place of a
        search list in turn.  With a system volume, STARTUP.COM defines
        SYS$SHARE as its [SYSTEM.SYSLIB] directory, where FTNRTL.EXE is;
        define it as a search list, in SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM say,
        to keep shareable images of your own on another volume too.
     3. The host: the host library directory (the vax.library setting),
        then the name as given, each as written and then in lower case.
     4. FTNRTL, which is built into govax.

   An image no rule finds stops the activation, as on VMS:

     %DCL-W-ACTIMAGE, error activating image MYSHR
     -CLI-E-IMAGEFNF, image file not found DUA1:[SYSLIB]MYSHR.EXE;

   (here with SYS$SHARE defined as DUA1:[SYSLIB]), and $STATUS
   %X100388B2.

   The image's ident and match control (LINK's GSMATCH= option) are
   checked against those the program was linked with.  The two match
   controls must agree, and then the program's decides: ALWAYS matches
   any ident, EQUAL needs the same major and minor ids, and LEQUAL the
   same major id and a minor id no lower than the program's.  A mismatch
   is %SYSTEM-F-SHRIDMISMAT, with the image's file named.  govax's own
   routines always match.

   A shareable image linked at a base address (BASE=) goes there, as
   does one the program's link placed (an executable linked with BASE=);
   any other goes at the end of P0's pages, the shareable image list
   taken from its end, so the program's last image is placed first.
   Then each image's fixups are done (its .ADDRESS list and its G^
   cells, and those of the program), and its pages take their
   protections.  Shareable images are private to the process: copied
   into memory, or mapped from their files with paging on
   (VMINIT/PAGING), never shared with another process.

   The debugger's SHOW IMAGE lists the images and the files they came
   from.

2 Qualifiers
/BREAK

   The same as /DEBUG.

/DEBUG

      /[NO]DEBUG

   /DEBUG runs the image under the debugger, which stops it at the first
   instruction of its main routine (after the entry mask) and prompts
   with DBG>.  GO continues it; STEP, SET BREAK, EXAMINE, and the rest
   are the debugger's commands.  An image linked /DEBUG starts the
   debugger the same way, unless /NODEBUG says not to.  An image linked
   /NOTRACEBACK has no symbols for the debugger, and runs without it.

/EXECUTE

      /[NO]EXECUTE

   /NOEXECUTE loads the image, and its shareable images, without
   running it.

/HOST

   After the filespec, makes it a host file, whatever it looks like.

/INIT

      /[NO]INIT

   Whether each shareable image's LIB$INITIALIZE routines run before
   the program.  The default is SET DEBUG's LIBINIT switch, which is on
   unless it has been turned off.

/STEP

   The same as /DEBUG.

2 Examples

   1.$ RUN HELLO

     Runs HELLO.EXE in the default directory.

   2.$ RUN/DEBUG DUA1:[PROGS]SORTER

     Runs SORTER.EXE under the debugger.

   3.$ RUN "hello.exe"/HOST

     Runs a host file.
